Check out OccAny: a super generalizable approach for 3D occupancy estimation for any sensor rig and any domain, without needing poses or intrinsics. Excellent work by @AnhQuanCAO and @tuan_hung_vu.

@AnhQuanCAO: "One model. Any sensor rig. Any domain. No poses. No intrinsics. **OccAny**: CVPR 2026 paper on generalized unconstrained urban 3D occupancy. Code release: training, data, eval & viz - OccAny+ (Depth Anything 3, SAM3), OccAny (MUSt3R, SAM2)." 

The linked paper discusses OccAny, the first generalized model for metric 3D occupancy from unconstrained scenes, leveraging foundation models like Depth-Anything-3 and SAM3 for enhanced 3D geometry.

What gets missed with AI productivity gains is that most roles will continue to be as sophisticated as the tools allow. The notion that today's jobs will be replaced with AI is a fallacy; the market is dynamic, and access to the same technology will lead to users raising their output levels, making prior job definitions irrelevant.

Those who understand their fields and grow their skills will continue to stand out. As productivity increases, individuals will tackle bigger and more complex problems, requiring expertise to fully complete tasks. For example, an engineer using AI will be far more productive than a non-engineer trying to build software, and basic tasks in various professions will evolve beyond their current definitions.

@andrewchen: "Hot take :) The biggest and most productive people in the AI era are the folks who are already good at their jobs. AI as a multiplier, not an equalizer/democratizer."

In ReDi, we showed that jointly modeling VAE latents and DINO features boosts diffusion. CoReDi goes one step further by learning the feature space instead of keeping it fixed, leading to better joint modeling.

CoReDi is 90 times faster than SiT and 13 times faster than REPA.

@ThKouz: "Introducing CoReDi: Coevolving Representations for Joint Image–Feature Diffusion. Joint diffusion boosts image generation by injecting semantic features. But one assumption goes unquestioned: the feature space is fixed. What if it was learned instead?"

